<<elseif _posePart is "latSpread">>
	<<if _posingLevel is 0>>
	You return to the mirror, thinking about the necessary practice for a bodybuilding competition. Best way to learn how to pose is practice, right? So you start taking your outerwear in order to see if you can nail a proper lat spread. <<if $muscle < 25>>You see your shoulder blades and confuse them for muscle for a second, getting a bit disappointed.<<elseif $muscle < 35>>Your back looks quite bare, with little muscle or volume to be seen.<<elseif $muscle < 45>>Your work is starting to pay off, as you see some bumps on your back, leading you to change to an overhead light to maximize the effect.<<elseif $muscle < 55>>Your back is looking quite strong already, feeling broader and bumpier, which makes you giddy.<<elseif $muscle < 65>>You impress yourself with your gains, watching a wide and muscular back of your torso by twisting your neck to the mirror.<<else>>You have to use your phone’s camera as an improvised mirror to truly appreciate the gigantic back muscles you developed on the mirror, inspiring you to make some social media poses as you are awed with the impressive physique being exhibited.<</if>> With the goods on full display, you start trying to do a proper lat spread.<br><br> Unfortunately, your lack of training is patently obvious. You try comically to lift your shoulders as you put your hands on your hips, but it looks more like you are pretending to be an angry mother than posing for bodybuilding. <<if $muscle < 25>>There really is nothing there to show, and you wonder why you even bothered taking your top off.<<elseif $muscle < 35>>You can kind of see SOMETHING, and you hope that at some point it will actually be worth showing.<<elseif $muscle < 45>>While you posing is downright awkward, there is definitely some muscle starting to develop, as your back feels broader and more solid.<<elseif $muscle < 55>>You surprise yourself with how wide your back is starting to look, though it’s probably going to look a lot better once you actually manage to engage these muscles properly.<<elseif $muscle < 65>>Dang girl, your posing may be crap, but these lats were made for flexing.<<else>>You wonder why you even need to bother to learn how to pose, if even with your complete inaptitude your back is so wide you aren’t even sure will still be able to get through small doors at this point.<</if>> You aren’t sure exactly what you were expecting: you have no clue about posing, and it’s rather obvious you have to study a lot.<br><br> With a shrug and a sigh, you decide to try something to measure what these muscles of yours can accomplish. You entertain the idea of trying a planche, that one exercise where you hold yourself off the ground with just your hands. You try to remember the guides you saw earlier, and it seemed quite daunting, but with training and conditioning, you decide to keep your hopes up. <<if $muscle < 25>>You put your hands on the ground, but even a basic push up is proving to be a bit challenging. You come to the realization it’s going to be a long road...<<elseif $muscle < 35>>Feeling a bit more confident, you decide to try a planche tuck, just lifting yourself with your legs bent in front of your chest. It might not look like the easiest move, but the reality is somehow even more daunting. You sort of get into the position, but just barely. You clearly need to work on your muscles a bit more before you can take this any further.<<elseif $muscle < 45>>You go for the planche tuck, and actually manage to hit the pose, holding it for what you believe is a good 10-15 seconds, though it feels like an eternity. It inspires you to keep at it, maybe soon you will get to your goal.<<elseif $muscle < 55>>You go for a planche tuck, which proves to be pretty manageable as you get to a nice half-minute in that position. So you go that next step and try a piked straddle planche, raising your hips and opening your legs to the sides. It’s not the easiest, but you manage. And so you try to see how long you can hold that pose until you move to the real deal.<<elseif $muscle < 65>>You finally manage to pull a very consistent piked straddle planche, so with bated breath, you go to the real deal. You slowly stretch your legs back, feeling the tension spread through your body. You tremble, trying to get that proper balance so you don’t fall. Seconds feel like years as your legs come together and finally… FINALLY… stretch all the way into a proper planche. You maintain the pose for a couple of seconds, still shaking, before settling back down.<<else>>Placing your hands firmly to support yourself, you calmly start engaging your core and legs in a proper planche. It’s not easy, but it’s also not hard. You stand… or rather, lie straight… proud of your incredible feat of strength, knowing the power you hold within this incredible body.<</if>> And so you get back up, and continue with your day.
	<<elseif _posingLevel is 1>>
	You return to the mirror, thinking about the necessary practice for a bodybuilding competition. Best way to learn how to pose is practice, right? So you start taking your outerwear in order to see if you can nail a proper lat spread. <<if $muscle < 25>>You see your shoulder blades and confuse them for muscle for a second, getting a bit disappointed.<<elseif $muscle < 35>>Your back looks quite bare, with little muscle or volume to be seen.<<elseif $muscle < 45>>Your work is starting to pay off, as you see some bumps on your back, leading you to change to an overhead light to maximize the effect.<<elseif $muscle < 55>>Your back is looking quite strong already, feeling broader and bumpier, which makes you giddy.<<elseif $muscle < 65>>You impress yourself with your gains, watching a wide and muscular back of your torso by twisting your neck to the mirror.<<else>>You have to use your phone’s camera as an improvised mirror to truly appreciate the gigantic back muscles you developed on the mirror, inspiring you to make some social media poses as you are awed with the impressive physique being exhibited.<</if>> With the goods on full display, you start trying to do a proper lat spread.<br><br> After some practice, you hope that you can NOT embarrass yourself at what you initially thought was going to be an easy pose to pull off. Turning around, you once again work to get that back on display. <<if $muscle < 25>>You still lack the muscle, but at least now you don’t feel like your posing is as ridiculous. Lots of improvement still ahead, though.<<elseif $muscle < 35>>You manage to sort of not fail miserably, as you can see SOME progress on your back, but your posing is still awkward and ineffective. Glad to see you put on some muscle, at least.<<elseif $muscle < 45>>A nice, broad back unfolds like an umbrella, but that’s more to your muscle’s credit than any decent ability at the pose.<<elseif $muscle < 55>>You surprise yourself with how big you are getting, though it also kind of feels like there’s a lot left on the table as you really cannot properly engage the muscles. You move your shoulders and elbows, but nothing seems to quite make it better.<<elseif $muscle < 65>>Your back is statuesque, with incredible volume and ridges… but your flex is still uneven and unstable.<<else>>You really don’t even need to flex or take your top off for anyone to notice you have a humongous back… which just makes more obvious that your muscle mass is years ahead of your ability to hold a proper lat spread.<</if>> You may be better than a complete beginner, but not much.<br><br> With a shrug and a sigh, you decide to try something to measure what these muscles of yours can accomplish. So it’s now time to try a planche, that one exercise where you hold yourself off the ground with just your hands. You try to remember the guides you saw earlier, and it seemed quite daunting, but with training and conditioning, you decide to keep your hopes up. <<if $muscle < 25>>You put your hands on the ground, but even a basic push up is proving to be a bit challenging. You come to the realization it’s going to be a long road...<<elseif $muscle < 35>>Feeling a bit more confident, you decide to try a planche tuck, just lifting yourself with your legs bent in front of your chest. It might not look like the easiest move, but the reality is somehow even more daunting. You sort of get into the position, but just barely. You clearly need to work on your muscles a bit more before you can take this any further.<<elseif $muscle < 45>>You go for the planche tuck, and actually manage to hit the pose, holding it for what you believe is a good 10-15 seconds, though it feels like an eternity. It inspires you to keep at it, maybe soon you will get to your goal.<<elseif $muscle < 55>>You go for a planche tuck, which proves to be pretty manageable as you get to a nice half-minute in that position. So you go that next step and try a piked straddle planche, raising your hips and opening your legs to the sides. It’s not the easiest, but you manage. And so you try to see how long you can hold that pose until you move to the real deal.<<elseif $muscle < 65>>You finally manage to pull a very consistent piked straddle planche, so with bated breath, you go to the real deal. You slowly stretch your legs back, feeling the tension spread through your body. You tremble, trying to get that proper balance so you don’t fall. Seconds feel like years as your legs come together and finally… FINALLY… stretch all the way into a proper planche. You maintain the pose for a couple of seconds, still shaking, before settling back down.<<else>>Placing your hands firmly to support yourself, you calmly start engaging your core and legs in a proper planche. It’s not easy, but it’s also not hard. You stand… or rather, lie straight… proud of your incredible feat of strength, knowing the power you hold within this incredible body.<</if>> And so you get back up, and continue with your day.
